---
// source: c5540529f7c2
import CaseLink from '../../components/CaseLink.astro';
import { euroRange, itemRange, resignation } from '../../content/examples';
import { euros } from '../../content/format';
import type { Lang } from '../../i18n/languages';

interface Props {
  lang: Lang;
}
const { lang } = Astro.props;

const salary = itemRange(resignation, 'pending_salary');
const holidays = itemRange(resignation, 'holiday_pay');
const extraPay = itemRange(resignation, 'extra_pay');
const deduction = itemRange(resignation, 'notice_deduction');
if (!salary || !holidays || !extraPay || !deduction) throw new Error('Incomplete example');
---

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="bv-que-lleva">What the final settlement after resignation includes</h3>
  <p>
    Handing in your resignation (baja voluntaria) means the contract ends by your decision (art.
    49.1.d ET). Even though you are the one leaving, what you have already earned and not yet been
    paid is due to you from the employer:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Pay for the days worked in the last month (art. 26 ET).</li>
    <li>Holiday accrued this year and not taken (art. 38 ET).</li>
    <li>
      The accrued part of the extra payments (pagas extra), if they are not spread over your monthly
      payslips (art. 31 ET).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    What there is not is severance pay: resignation does not give rise to it (art. 49.1.d ET). All
    the figures are gross, before tax withholding and contributions.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>49.1.d · 26 · 31 · 38</span> ET
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="bv-preaviso">The notice period in your collective agreement</h3>
  <p>
    When you resign, you have to give the notice period set by your collective agreement (convenio)
    or by local custom (art. 49.1.d ET). If your collective agreement provides for it and you do not
    give it, the employer can deduct the missing days from the final settlement, at your daily pay
    rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    The calculator asks you how many days your collective agreement sets and how many you gave, and
    gives the maximum deduction. If you do not know your collective agreement’s figure, that
    deduction comes out as “cannot be checked”.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>49.1.d</span> ET
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="bv-ejemplo">Worked example</h3>
  <p>
    A person earning 1.500&nbsp;€ gross a month, with two extra payments of 1.500&nbsp;€ that accrue
    by half-year, works from 1 March 2023 to 15 October 2026. This year they have taken 10 of their
    22 working days of holiday. Their collective agreement sets 15 days’ notice and they leave
    without giving it. These are the calculator’s figures:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      October pay: {euroRange(salary, lang)}, depending on whether the month is counted as 30 days
      or 31.
    </li>
    <li>Untaken holiday: {euroRange(holidays, lang)}.</li>
    <li>Christmas extra payment accrued since July: {euroRange(extraPay, lang)}.</li>
    <li>Severance pay: {euros(0)}.</li>
    <li>Deduction for the notice not given: at most {euros(deduction.max)}.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="example">
    <span class="example__label">The sums</span> The range for each item comes from counting by days
    or by months, as employers do. The deduction is 15 days at {euros(deduction.max / 15)} a day,
    the higher of the two daily pay rates: monthly pay divided by 30 or annual pay divided by 365.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="bv-paro">And unemployment benefit</h3>
  <p>
    A resignation is not a legal situation of unemployment, so, as a general rule, it does not give
    you unemployment benefit (paro) (art. 267.2.a LGSS). There are exceptions, such as leaving
    because of a relocation or serious non-payment. You will find them in{' '}
    <CaseLink lang={lang} id="benefit_resignation" />.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>267</span> LGSS
  </p>
</section>
